  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"All games must stop with enough time to pack before the room closes, and each table agrees how to record an unfinished game if desired.\", \"decision\": \"How should the evening’s final stopping time be communicated?\", \"goal\": \"Help guests find compatible games, understand how to play, and enjoy the evening without avoidable waiting or confusion.\", \"problem\": \"A community room is hosting an evening of board games for adults with different levels of experience. Several tables, a host, willing game teachers, short waiting activities, and a varied collection are available. Guests can decline any game and join a later one. Every game has an agreed latest stopping time before the room closes, and ordinary refreshments and conversation have their own small areas.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nEach table writes its final stopping time on a visible card and assigns a player to announce the approaching deadline. The limit stays in view and its reminder comes from someone already following that table’s progress.\nThe host displays one room closing countdown and announces the final packing period to all tables together. A common signal coordinates the end of games that began at different times while preserving space for component checks.\nThe host gives the final stopping time before each game starts and returns with a reminder shortly beforehand. Players hear the limit when committing to play and receive a timely prompt to prepare for packing."
    }
  ],
